WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.482
A domain name provides a human-readable label within the Domain Name System.

00:00:05.482 --> 00:00:09.746
A registered domain can contain subdomains such as app.

00:00:09.746 --> 00:00:10.761
example.

00:00:10.761 --> 00:00:12.588
com or mail.

00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:13.603
example.

00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:14.619
com.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:20.101
Organizations use naming to organize services, environments, regions, products, and third-party integrations.

00:00:20.101 --> 00:00:26.395
Registrars manage registration relationships, while name servers publish authoritative DNS information for a domain.

00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:29.440
Administrative arrangements can change over time.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:37.359
Public registration details may be privacy-protected, incomplete, or maintained by a service provider rather than the operational team.

00:00:37.359 --> 00:00:44.059
Naming patterns can help defenders recognize likely services or environments, but labels are not proof.

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:50.759
A name containing dev may host production traffic, remain unused, or point to a vendor.

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:55.429
Analysts should document the literal name separately from any interpretation.

00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:59.693
Abandoned or forgotten names create operational and brand risk.

00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:03.551
Records can outlive projects, vendors, or cloud resources.

00:01:03.551 --> 00:01:12.281
A stale name may confuse customers, expose old documentation, or point toward infrastructure no longer governed by the expected owner.

00:01:12.281 --> 00:01:14.515
Domain data is time-sensitive.

00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:18.778
Registrations, name servers, DNS answers, and ownership arrangements change.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:21.824
Preserve observation dates and historical context.

00:01:21.824 --> 00:01:28.930
A current lookup should not be used to rewrite what was true during an earlier event.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:35.224
Use domain information to form defensible questions: Is the asset in the internal inventory?

00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:37.864
Is there an assigned owner?

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.503
Is the record still needed?

00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:48.422
Confirm through authorized organizational records before labeling a domain as rogue, compromised, or controlled by a specific party.
